In news:<Ykeve.12809$eM6.5899@newsread3.news.atl.earthlink.net>, Simmie 
Simmerson <radiohead3@earthlink.net> says...
> [...]
> Mostly I play wizards because I prefer casting spells.

Remember that spell casting uses up your nutrition and hence causes your 
hunger to increase faster. This is not a problem for wizards with very 
high Int, since there are special rules protecting them. A special 
exception is the 'detect food' spell, which does not cause hunger.

> [...]
> My biggest problem so far is starvation,

First, see above. Second, don't go burdened (or above). Third, don't 
wear jewelry (rings/amulets) if you have a food problem.

> or having monsters molest me to death while I'm unconscious.

Unconscious because of starvation? Don't let it happen.
'Unconscious'/paralysed because of a creature? Some creatures should not 
be attacked in direct combat.

> Praying only works so many times,

It works an infinite number of times. However there is a semi-random 
timeout period, your god will be angry if you pray before this has 
passed. It is possible to keep a character alive virtually forever 
through praying. Turn on the 'turn counter display' in the status bar, 
and try to find a number of turns to wait between prayers that seem 
safe. A not as good solution is to only pray when you start to faint, 
but then you will get in trouble while unconscious sooner or later.

Note that this works if you don't have any abnormal nutrition needs. 
Remember what was said above - easy on the spellcasting, don't go 
burdened, careful with the jewelry.

> and my pet seems to beat me to the edible corpses.

You don't *need* to bring your pet, but for a wizard it could be useful 
to have some fighting help. One of the prices to pay for keeping pets is 
that they need to, and will, consume food that you could have use for 
yourself. Many people don't keep pets at all, many like playing with 
pets. My personal recommendation would be to keep your pet in the 
beginning (10 levels or so, you will notice when it isn't of much help 
anymore) and learn how to handle your hunger anyway.

> In any case, food is the problem I'm faced with. 

I don't think it will be a problem anymore if you keep the above in 
mind. I had great troubles with food/hunger in my early Nethack days, 
nowadays it's a problem for perahsp one character in a hundred, and then 
only for a short while.

> There's a few things I'd like to do here...
> One, train my pet. Somehow. I need tripe rations, but even when I somehow 
> manage to stumble across a food shop, my pet eats the tripe rations off the 
> floor before I can get to them. Stupid little bastard!

Dogs and cats will abandon almost any other task to gobble down a tripe 
ration in their sight. See to it that you are the first on into shops, 
and close the door behind you, so you can get the tripe rations 
undisturbed.

> Anyway, is stealing from shops all you can train your pets to do?

Well, that is a matter of how you define the word 'train'. Your pets 
will gain levels (until their creature types max level) by fighting and 
eating. When they are stronger, they will dare attack stronger enemies. 
Your pets can help you discover properties of items (I don't want to be 
too spoilerish about this). By polymorphing a pet you can get another, 
perhaps more useful and powerful pet. Some types of pets are able to 
wield weapons, etc.

> Two, find a way to NOT starve. Either way I'm screwed, because I try not to 
> delve too much into the dungeon because I level slowly on purpose (I let the 
> pet fight most of the time, and they get pretty strong, for kittens!)...

Uou've seen that they level up after a while perhaps? The kitten becomes 
a housecat, and later the housecat grows into a large cat. Same thing 
for dogs and horses (and even other stranger pet creatures).
Try a class that come with a pet horse some time, even stronger than 
cats/dogs, and they will not compete so much for the same food as you 
(horses mainly eat lichen corpses and some other special food).

> but if I go down in search of food, I am beset by ever more powerful
> beasties. 

It is important to try to keep a balance here. Many people would say 
that trying to advance your experience level at about the same pace as 
the dungeon level is a good general rule. Perhaps your combat tactics 
need improvement, or it's just that you consume food too fast that 
forces you into combat situations that you can't handle.

> Then I have Anhur getting miffed at me... hey, God, I'm only trying to find 
> YOUR stupid amulet! Think you could feed me once in a while, or is that 
> asking too much!?

Figure out a proper number of turns to wait between prayers, to pray 
safely.

> Anyway, I don't think I've gotten past level 5 as far as dungeon levels go. 
> Maybe 6. The poop really hits the fan when those giant ants and bees come 
> for me, but I can usually play and instrument and scare them off...

Interesting tactic. The normal strategies against 'swarm' monsters 
(ants, bees, orcs, etc) on early levels is to choose *where* and *when* 
to fight them. Examples - use a bottleneck on the level to only fight 
onw at a time and be able to retreat when you need to heal, go back up a 
level and return when you're better equipped or healed, use your pet to 
do the fighting (it can fight much better at you in the early levels), 
use distance weapons and/or spells.

> Anyway, in my last game I got my strength and constitution up to 15, and my 
> wisdom to 13 (started with 10).

You shouldn't bother too much about increasing your stats in the 
beginning, it isn't very important. Watch out if you notice some 
behaviour of yours that causes them to lower though.

> I think I have strength figured out. Walk around stressed, easy enough.

ARGH!!! Don't do that! Try to never even be burdened, and only ever be 
burdened if you feel safe and have plenty of food.
You will consume food much faster per turn, and comparing the food vs 
movement ratio will be even worse since you also move much slower.
Not too mention that being burdened (and above) in a fight will let the 
monsters get in many more hits on you for every hit you make. And you 
will not be able to run from mosnters either. And you won't be able to 
get too food before your pet etc etc etc.
This is a source too many of your problems.

> I think I boosted wisdom by finding secret 
> pathways, but I am not sure. I have no idea why constitution went up. How 
> about intelligence, dexterity and charisma? Any tips on bringing those up?

Don't bother too much about 'exercising' stats at this time. Int and Cha 
can't be exercised as the other stats. Keep focused on surviving fights, 
not starving, and improving your equipment.

> Also, can someone tell me how to control my pet better? It wanders off 
> sometimes, eats food in shops, that sort of thing. What can be done with 
> pets?

Don't be slow (-> don't carry too much), and you will be able to get to 
places before your pet, close doors so it doesn't get where you don't 
want it, block it with your body, etc.
There are a number of tools that can be of use to control pets too. 
Pretty obvious ones. One of these tools has a magical version that is 
very useful, and not rare to find. (I don't want to spoil too much 
here.)

